1 TITL King Li of Zhou
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Taken from http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_Li_of_Zhou
2 CONT
2 CONT King Li of Zhou (died in 828 BC) (Chinese: 周厲王; pinyin: Zhōu Lì Wáng) was the tenth king of the Chinese Zhou Dynasty. Estimated dates of his reign are 877–841 BC or 857–842 BC (Cambridge History of Ancient China).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT King Li was a corrupt and decadent king. To pay for his pleasures and vices, King Li raised taxes and caused misery among his subjects. He enstated a new law which allowed him to punish anyone, by death, who dared to speak against
2 CONC  him. King Li’s bad rule soon forced many peasants and soldiers into revolt, and Li was sent into exile at a place called Zhi near Linfen (842 BC). His son was taken by one of his ministers and hidden.[2] When Li died in exile in 
2 CONC 828 BC, power was passed to his son.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources[edit source] 1.^ Feng, Li (2006), Landscape and Power in Early China: The Crisis and Fall of the Western Zhou 1045–771 BC, Cambridge University Press, ISBN 978-0-521-85272-2. 2.^ Sources of Western Zhou History: Inscribed 
2 CONC Bronze Vessels by Edward L. Shaughnessy 3.^ Sima Qian. Records of the Grand Historian 4.
